{"id":780,"date":"2020-11-04T12:38:13","date_gmt":"2020-11-04T20:38:13","guid":{"rendered":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/?post_type=lesson&#038;p=780"},"modified":"2023-08-21T08:41:35","modified_gmt":"2023-08-21T15:41:35","slug":"user-input","status":"publish","type":"lesson","link":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/user-input\/","title":{"rendered":"2D: User Input"},"content":{"rendered":"<div class=\"wpb-content-wrapper\"><p>[vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22167c0ca14\" name=\"6a22167c0ca14\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 1<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Input<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]In the previous lesson, you use conditionals to check the value of a variable and make a decision. The variables had to be changed manually in the program. What if there was a way to interact with the program and change its outcome without needing to rewrite the program? Getting user input will let you set a variable value by typing it in during runtime.[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22167c0cb4e\" name=\"6a22167c0cb4e\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 2<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Creating a menu<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">One of the best ways to see conditionals in action is by creating a menu of flight movements. From there, a user can enter a number using the \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">input()<\/code>\u00a0<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">function. The drone will then fly based on the code for a certain number. For example, after the number 3 is entered, the drone will fly in a triangle.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">This is one example of a menu. After a command is run, the menu will display again until \u201cExit\u201d is selected:<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">Menu:\r\n\r\nSpin in place\u00a0\r\nFly in a right angle\r\nFly in a triangle\r\nFly in a square\r\nExit<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22167c0cbcd\" name=\"6a22167c0cbcd\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 3<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Make your own menu<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Ready to make your own? Import the CoDrone Mini, direction, sequence, and time libraries, and then create the drone object and pair your drone. Next, set up a while loop so that while true, the menu will be printed.\u00a0<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Inside the while loop, include print commands for a \u201cMenu\u201d and then options for different maneuvers that you would like your CoDrone Mini to do. This can be anything, but you should have an idea of how to program each maneuver you include! Make sure one of those options is \u201cQuit\u201d, which will end the while loop.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Your menu should look something like this:<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">import CoDrone_mini\r\n\r\nfrom CoDrone_mini import Direction\r\n\r\nfrom CoDrone_mini import Sequence\r\n\r\nimport time\r\n\r\ndrone = CoDrone_mini.CoDrone()\r\n\r\ndrone.pair()\r\n\r\n\r\nwhile True:\r\n\r\n    print(\"Menu: \")\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 print(\"1. Spin Around \\n\"\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0    \"2. Flip\\n\"\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\"3. Sway\\n\"\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\"4. Square\\n\"\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\"5. Quit\\n\")<\/pre>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Wondering what the \u00a0<\/span><code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">\\n<\/code> <span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">is for? Without this, the menu could print very differently, like in one big line of code instead of in multiple statements in a block. Remember to line up the following lines at the next indent as shown so you don&#8217;t get an indentation error!<\/span><\/p>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22167c0cc54\" name=\"6a22167c0cc54\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 4<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Resetting and user input<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Your program needs to be able to read user input and determine which set of code to execute. You will use if and else if statements to check if the user input is \u201c1\u201d, \u201c2\u201d, \u201c3\u201d, \u201c4\u201d, or \u201c5\u201d.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Make sure you\u2019re still in the while loop, and then set your drone\u2019s roll, pitch, yaw, and throttle to 0. That way, they will all be ready for each maneuver you program! Next, create a variable called <\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">option<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\"> to store <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">input()<\/code><\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">. In the parameters for <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">input()<\/code><\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">, <\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">write<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\"> &#8220;Select an option: &#8220;. <\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">This will print the command, and when the user inputs a number, it will be stored as an option and called later on.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">This is what you should have now:<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">drone.set_throttle(0)\r\n\r\n    drone.set_roll(0)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.set_yaw(0)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.set_pitch(0)\r\n\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 option = input(\"Select an option: \")<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22167c0ccd3\" name=\"6a22167c0ccd3\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 5<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Making decisions<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Depending on the user input, your CoDrone will fly a different pattern! The best way to set this up is with conditionals. Have your drone take off, and then for option 1, set up an if statement and include your drone\u2019s maneuver.\u00a0<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">drone.takeoff()\r\n\r\n    if option == \"1\":\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0    drone.set_yaw(30)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.move(2)<\/pre>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">For options 2 through 4, use else if statements, and then include drone maneuvers.<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">    elif option == \"2\":\r\n        drone.flip(Direction.FORWARD)\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\r\n\r\n    elif option == \"3\":\r\n\r\n        drone.fly_sequence(Sequence.SWAY)\r\n\r\n    elif option == \"4\":\r\n\r\n        drone.fly_sequence(Sequence.SQUARE)<\/pre>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">For option 5, use another else if statement again, but include a print command that says, \u201cYou have quit the menu\u201d and then a break. This will exit the loop.<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">    elif option == \"5\":\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0    print(\"You have quit the menu.\")\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 break<\/pre>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Next, include an else statement that will work when an invalid user input (like a number outside of 1-5) is entered.<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">    else:\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0    print(\"Not a valid input. Try again.\")<\/pre>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Finally, have your drone land and then (this is optional) include a print command that says \u201cDone.\u201d<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">    drone.land()\r\n\r\nprint(\"Done\")<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22167c0cd29\" name=\"6a22167c0cd29\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 6<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Final code<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Your final program should look something like this:<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">import CoDrone_mini\r\n\r\nfrom CoDrone_mini import Direction\r\n\r\nfrom CoDrone_mini import Sequence\r\n\r\nimport time\r\n\r\ndrone = CoDrone_mini.CoDrone()\r\n\r\ndrone.pair()\r\n\r\n\r\nwhile True:\r\n\r\n    print(\"Menu: \")\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 print(\"1. Spin Around \\n\"\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0    \"2. Flip\\n\"\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\"3. Sway\\n\"\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\"4. Square\\n\"\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\"5. Quit\\n\")\r\n\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.set_throttle(0)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.set_roll(0)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.set_yaw(0)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.set_pitch(0)\r\n\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 option = input(\"Select an option: \")\r\n\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.takeoff()\r\n\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 if option == \"1\":\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0    drone.set_yaw(30)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.move(2)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 elif option == \"2\":\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.flip(Direction.FORWARD)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 elif option == \"3\":\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0    drone.fly_sequence(Sequence.SWAY)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 elif option == \"4\":\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.fly_sequence(Sequence.SQUARE)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 elif option == \"5\":\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 print(\"You have quit the menu.\")\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 break\r\n\r\n    else:\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 print(\"Not a valid input. Try again.\")\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.land()\r\n\r\nprint(\"Done\")<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22167c0cd9a\" name=\"6a22167c0cd9a\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 7<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Remote control<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">User input is also great for remote controlling your drone! In this activity, you will program your keyboard to act as a remote, with \u201cwasd\u201d and \u201cijkl\u201d as the joysticks.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">First, import the CoDrone Mini and time libraries, and then create the drone object and pair your drone.\u00a0<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Next, include print commands for \u201c<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Command options<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">\u201d and then for each of the eight flight directions: positive and negative roll, pitch, yaw, and throttle. Make sure to include a \u201c<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">quit<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">\u201d option, which will land the drone.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Your menu should look something like this:<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">import CoDrone_mini\r\n\r\nimport time\r\n\r\ndrone = CoDrone_mini.CoDrone()\r\n\r\ndrone.pair()\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nprint(\"Command Options: \")\r\n\r\nprint(\"w: throttle up \\n\"\r\n\r\n    \"s: throttle down\\n\"\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\"a: yaw left\\n\"\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\"d: yaw right\\n\"\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\"i: pitch forward\\n\"\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\"k: pitch backward\\n\"\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\"j: roll left\\n\"\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\"l: roll right\\n\"\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\"q: quit\")<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22167c0cdf5\" name=\"6a22167c0cdf5\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 8<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Takeoff<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Have your drone take off, and then set your trim if needed. This is for if your drone is drifting and you want to fix its pitch or roll, so if you\u2019re not having that issue, you can skip this step.\u00a0<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Next, create a variable called power and set its value to 40. This way, the default power will be 40%.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Your code should look like this:<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">drone.takeoff()\r\n\r\ndrone.set_trim(0,0) # Adjust for your drone\r\n\r\npower = 40<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22167c0ce5d\" name=\"6a22167c0ce5d\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 9<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>While loop<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Set up a while loop so that while true, the program can take user input to make decisions. Next, reset the roll, pitch, yaw, and throttle to 0.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Create a variable called <\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">direction<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\"> to store <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">input()<\/code><\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">. In the parameters for <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">input()<\/code><\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">, <\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">write<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\"> &#8220;Input a command: &#8220;. <\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">This will print the command, and when the user inputs a letter, it will be stored as an option and called later on.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Your code should look like this:<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">while True:\r\n\r\n    drone.set_throttle(0)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.set_roll(0)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.set_yaw(0)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.set_pitch(0)\r\n\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 direction = input(\"Input a command: \")<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22167c0cee2\" name=\"6a22167c0cee2\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 10<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Making decisions<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Just like the user input menu, you need to use if, else if, and else statements to have your drone fly in different directions based on the user input. For the w key, use an if statement and then include a command for your drone to throttle up. In the parameters, use the power variable, which will have the drone move at 40% power.<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">    if direction == \"w\":\r\n\r\n        drone.set_throttle(power)<\/pre>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">For the other 7 flight commands, use else if statements. If you want the drone to go to the left, down, or backwards, put a negative sign next to the power variable in the parameters.<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0elif direction == \"s\":\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0    drone.set_throttle(-power)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0elif direction == \"a\":\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0drone.set_yaw(-power)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0elif direction == \"d\":\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0drone.set_yaw(power)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0elif direction == \"i\":\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0drone.set_pitch(power)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0elif direction == \"k\":\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0drone.set_pitch(-power)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0elif direction == \"j\":\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0drone.set_roll(-power)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0elif direction == \"l\":\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0drone.set_roll(power)\r\n<\/pre>\n<p>Write one more else if statement for the quit option. This will wait so the landing command isn\u2019t skipped, land the drone, and break out of the loop.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">    elif direction == \"q\":\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0    time.sleep(0.05)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0drone.land()\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0break<\/pre>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Include an option that will let the user know they made a mistake if they input something that isn\u2019t an option in the program.\u00a0<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">    else:\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0    print(\"Not a command\")<\/pre>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Finally, include a delay and a move command with one second in the parameters. This is what will actually move the drone, along with the code you wrote for each key!<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">    time.sleep(0.05)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.move(1)<\/pre>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Outside of the loop, include a print command so you know when you\u2019ve exited the program.<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">print(\"Done\")<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22167c0cf45\" name=\"6a22167c0cf45\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 11<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Challenge<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<b>Challenge: Super Program<\/b><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">So you have a program that uses numbers to have your CoDrone Mini do different maneuvers, and you have another program that uses letters to have your CoDrone Mini fly in different directions. What if you combined them both in the same program?<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Rules:<\/span><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Must include an option to quit<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Must include a print command that lets the user know when they input an option that\u2019s not available<\/span><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row]<\/p>\n<\/div>","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"[vc_row][vc_column] Step 12 Input [vc_column_text]In the previous lesson, you use conditionals to check the 